{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"04","section":"05","chapter_title":"AI Conversations as a Teacher","title":"Tone, Bias, and Audience","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting tone, bias, and audience to a tone, bias, and audience edit table, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Classify loaded language into the four bias patterns (unobservable cause, person label, false urgency, audience mismatch) and select the matching repair move.","Apply the observability, behavior, and audience checks to flag and replace audience-wrong words, naming the reader before scanning.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher","cat100_supplement_tone_bias_and_audience","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/04/04"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_4_5_tone_bias_and_audience_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_4_5_tone_bias_and_audience_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_4_5_tone_bias_and_audience_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_4_5_tone_bias_and_audience_calibration"]},"content":"# Tone, Bias, and Audience\n\n![Tone, Bias, and Audience textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/04-05-tone-bias-and-audience.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nBias in a message usually hides at the word level, not the paragraph level. A draft can have a fine structure and still carry words that assume a cause you cannot know (\"obviously did not study\"), label a person instead of a behavior (\"lazy\"), or pressure a reader who has done nothing wrong (\"immediately\"). This section teaches you to scan a draft word by word, flag the loaded or audience-wrong terms, and log a specific replacement for each, so the edit is a visible decision rather than a vibe.\n\nThe reason to work at the word level is that this is where you can be precise and accountable. \"Make it less harsh\" is not a checkable instruction. \"Replace 'lazy habits' with 'could use more practice on fractions' because the original labels the child\" is. A find-and-replace log turns tone work into a list of decisions someone else can audit and agree or disagree with.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Classify loaded language into the four bias patterns (unobservable cause, person label, false urgency, audience mismatch) and select the matching repair move.\n- Apply the observability, behavior, and audience checks to flag and replace audience-wrong words, naming the reader before scanning.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nAn AI (a general-purpose assistant such as ChatGPT, Claude, or Copilot) drafts a note to parents whose children failed a quiz: \"Your child obviously did not study and failed the quiz badly. These lazy habits will hurt them. They need to fix this immediately.\" Three words carry the damage. \"Obviously did not study\" asserts a cause the writer cannot observe. \"Lazy habits\" labels the child rather than naming a behavior. \"Immediately\" applies pressure that does not match a single quiz.\n\nA word-level scan replaces each one with something observable and proportional: the assumed cause becomes a fact (\"scored 4 out of 10\"), the label becomes a behavior (\"could use more practice on fractions\"), and the pressure becomes a reasonable window (\"over the next two weeks\"). The structure barely changes; the words do all the harm and all the repair.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nLoaded language falls into a few repeatable categories. Recognizing the category tells you how to fix it.\n\n| Bias pattern | What it does | Repair move |\n|--------------|--------------|-------------|\n| Unobservable cause | claims a reason the writer cannot know (motivation, effort) | replace with what you can observe (a score, a count) |\n| Person label | judges the person, not the action (\"lazy,\" \"careless\") | name the specific behavior instead |\n| False urgency | applies pressure out of proportion to the stakes | set a realistic timeframe |\n| Audience mismatch | uses words wrong for the reader (jargon for a parent) | match the reader's vocabulary |\n\nThe deepest of these is the unobservable cause, because it is the easiest to write without noticing. A writer cannot see whether a child studied; they can only see a score. Swapping the inferred cause for the observed fact is both fairer and more useful, since the parent can act on a fact but cannot act on an accusation. Two research lines back the move: writers default to dispositional explanations they cannot verify (the correspondence bias; Gawronski, 2004), and behavior-specific feedback research shows naming the observable behavior is more actionable than a trait label (Sutherland, Wehby, & Copeland, 2000).\n\nAudience is the frame that decides whether a word is wrong. \"Quotient\" is fine for a colleague and wrong for a parent with no math background. So the scan is not \"find harsh words\" in the abstract; it is \"find words wrong for this reader,\" which means you must name the reader before you scan.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A note to a parent reads: ’Your child is just not a math person yet.’ It sounds gentle. Which bias pattern is still hiding in it?\" explanation=\"’Not a math person’ judges who the child is (a fixed trait) rather than naming a behavior, so it fails the behavior check. Politeness does not remove the label; the word still assigns an identity the parent cannot act on. The fix names an observable behavior instead, such as ’missed 6 of 10 fraction problems.’\" options='[{\"text\":\"Person label: it judges the child as a fixed type instead of naming a behavior\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"False urgency: it pressures the parent to act now\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Audience mismatch: ’math person’ is jargon a parent cannot read\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No bias; a gentle, polite sentence is automatically fair\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nTone and bias work is concrete word-level editing: you scan a draft for loaded or audience-wrong words and log a find-and-replace decision for each one. A freshman does this as a two-column edit table so every change is visible. Follow the steps.\n\nStep 1. The AI drafted this note for parents of students who failed a quiz. Current AI drafts (as of 2026) still tend to over-assert causes and reach for templated urgency, which is exactly why the word-level scan matters:\n\n```text\nYour child obviously did not study and failed the quiz badly.\nThese lazy habits will hurt them. They need to fix this immediately.\n```\n\nStep 2. Scan for loaded words and audience mismatches. Log each FIND with the reason and a REPLACE:\n\n| Find (loaded word) | Why it is a problem | Replace with |\n|--------------------|---------------------|--------------|\n| \"obviously did not study\" | assumes a cause you cannot know | \"scored 4/10 on Tuesday's quiz\" |\n| \"lazy habits\" | labels the child, blames | \"could use more practice on fractions\" |\n| \"immediately\" | pressuring tone for parents | \"over the next two weeks\" |\n\nStep 3. Apply the replacements to produce the audience-fit version:\n\n```text\nYour child scored 4 out of 10 on Tuesday's fractions quiz. With a\nlittle more practice on fractions over the next two weeks, this is\nvery fixable. Here are two things you can try at home.\n```\n\nStep 4. Log the one rejected change. The AI also suggested replacing \"your child\" with the student's first name everywhere. You rejected it because in a privacy-sensitive parent email the relationship word \"your child\" is clearer and safer than repeating the name. The find-and-replace table plus that rejection is the evidence: a reader can see every word you changed and why.\n\nPut the word-level edit into the order that keeps each replacement defensible:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Name the reader first (a parent with no math background), since audience decides which words are wrong\", \"Scan the draft word by word and flag each loaded or audience-wrong term\", \"Label each flagged word with its bias pattern (unobservable cause, person label, false urgency, audience mismatch)\", \"Log a concrete replacement for each flag and the reason in a find-and-replace table\", \"Apply the replacements and re-read for over-correction into vagueness\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nNow repair the failed-quiz note one flagged word at a time, predicting each fix before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Scan and replace the loaded words\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The draft says ’your child obviously did not study.’ Which bias pattern is this, and what replaces it?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Unobservable cause: replace with what you can see, ’scored 4/10 on Tuesday’s quiz’\",\"Audience mismatch: replace with a simpler synonym for ’study’\",\"False urgency: add a deadline\",\"No problem: ’obviously’ just adds emphasis\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Unobservable cause. You cannot see whether the child studied; you can only see the score. Swapping the inferred cause for the observed fact is both fairer and something the parent can act on.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The draft says ’these lazy habits.’ Which fix passes the behavior check?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Name the behavior: ’could use more practice on fractions’\",\"Soften it to ’somewhat lazy habits’\",\"Keep it but add ’please don’t take this personally’\",\"Delete the whole sentence and say nothing\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Name the behavior. ’Lazy’ labels the child; ’could use more practice on fractions’ names an action the parent can support. Softening the label still leaves a label.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Finally, ’immediately’ is false urgency for a single quiz, so replace it with a proportional window such as ’over the next two weeks.’ You reject the AI’s offer to swap ’your child’ for the student’s first name, because in a parent email the relationship word is clearer and safer for privacy. Every flagged word now has a logged replacement and a reason a reviewer could check.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nReplace inferred causes with observed facts, labels with behaviors, and pressure with proportion. A message is audience-fit when every flagged word has a logged replacement and a reason a reviewer could check.\n\nRun these three checks on each flagged word:\n\n1. **Observability check:** Does the word state something you can actually see (a score, a date), or does it assume an inner cause (effort, attitude)?\n2. **Behavior check:** Does the word name what the person did, or does it label who they are?\n3. **Audience check:** Is the word right for this specific reader's vocabulary and stakes, or is it borrowed from a different audience?\n\nIf a word claims to know why someone acted, it almost certainly fails the observability check and should be replaced with what you observed.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is thinking polite words equal unbiased words. \"Unfortunately your child is simply not a math person\" is polite and still carries a fixed-trait label that the observability and behavior checks both reject. Courtesy does not remove bias; specificity does.\n\nThe second misreading is over-correcting into vagueness. Stripping every strong word can leave a mush that says nothing (\"there may be some areas to look at\"). The goal is not blandness; it is replacing an unfair word with a precise one. \"Scored 4/10\" is both strong and fair.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the scan to a recommendation a student asks AI to write about a peer. The draft says: \"She is a natural genius who never has to try, unlike most students.\" Scan it. \"Natural genius who never has to try\" is an unobservable-cause claim and, oddly, a backhanded one, since it implies she does not work. \"Unlike most students\" is an audience mismatch in a recommendation, where comparing down to others reads as unkind. Replace the first with an observed behavior (\"she revised her project three times after feedback\") and cut the comparison entirely.\n\nThe rewrite reads: \"She revised her project three times after feedback, and each version was clearer than the last.\" It is shorter, it names what she did, and it removes both the trait label and the comparison. The walkthrough shows that even praise can carry bias: \"genius\" sounds flattering but assumes a cause and erases the effort that the observed behavior actually credits her for.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **flagged word** on the left, **category and replacement** on the right. A strong reader does not write \"this sounds harsh\"; they write \"'lazy' is a person-label, replace with the behavior 'could use more fraction practice.'\" Naming the category makes the fix repeatable.\n\nA strong reader of Tone, Bias, and Audience can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which words assume a cause the writer cannot observe?\n2. Which words label the person instead of naming a behavior?\n3. Which words are wrong for this specific reader, and what would fit?\n4. Which strong word should stay because it is precise rather than loaded?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when a reviewer can read your find-and-replace table and judge each swap independently, because every row names the flagged word, the bias category, and the replacement. Keep the original draft verbatim so the reader can see what each replacement is responding to.\n\nA useful closing note for the artifact would read: \"I replaced 'obviously did not study' with the observed score because I cannot know why the child scored low. I rejected the AI's suggestion to use the student's first name throughout, because in a parent email the relationship word 'your child' is both clearer and safer for privacy.\" That ties one swap to the observability check and one rejection to an audience-and-privacy reason.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the scan from a parent email to a public web page, since CAT 100 students publish to GitHub Pages. On a public page the audience is unknown and permanent, so the audience check tightens: a word that assumes shared context (\"as everyone knows\") fails because not every visitor shares it, and any word naming a real person raises a privacy flag the moment the page is public. The categories transfer, but the audience-mismatch check becomes stricter because you cannot predict or take back who reads a published page. Understanding the method means raising the bar for the new, wider audience rather than reusing the parent-email standard.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how word-level bias scanning connects to the chapter’s wider ideas:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher\", \"cat100_supplement_tone_bias_and_audience\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the tone, bias, and audience edit table packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/04-05-tone-bias-and-audience-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"tone, bias, and audience edit table\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"04.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the tone, bias, and audience edit table.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the tone, bias, and audience edit table for CAT 100 04.05. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_tone_bias_and_audience\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your edit table flags each loaded word, names its bias category, logs a concrete replacement, and records one rejected change with a reason.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE Career Readiness Competencies (2024): Communication; Equity & Inclusion](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE Standards for Students 1.2 Digital Citizen, indicator 1.2.b (positive, safe, legal, ethical technology use)](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- Gawronski, B. (2004). Theory-based bias correction in dispositional inference. European Review of Social Psychology. https://doi.org/10.1080/10463280440000026\n- Sutherland, K. S., Wehby, J. H., & Copeland, S. R. (2000). Effect of varying rates of behavior-specific praise. Journal of Emotional and Behavioral Disorders. https://doi.org/10.1177/106342660000800101\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_5_tone_bias_and_audience_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_tone_bias_and_audience","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_tone_bias_and_audience","cat100_supplement_ai_conversations_as_a_teacher"],"stem":"Your edit table flags the phrase 'lazy habits.' Which replacement removes the label while keeping the message?","options":["careless attitude","could use more practice on fractions","bad student behavior","obviously unmotivated"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"It describes the behavior to work on instead of labeling the child's character.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_5_label_the_person","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_5_tone_bias_and_audience_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_tone_bias_and_audience","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_tone_bias_and_audience"],"stem":"The draft says 'Your child obviously did not study.' Why does your edit table flag 'obviously did not study'?","options":["It is too short.","It states a cause you cannot actually know as if it were a fact.","It uses the word 'your.'","It is grammatically incorrect."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"You can observe the score, not the cause. Replace the assumed cause with the observable fact.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_5_assumed_cause","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_5_tone_bias_and_audience_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_tone_bias_and_audience"],"stem":"The AI suggests replacing 'your child' with the student's first name throughout a parent email. What is the strongest evidence-based response?","options":["Accept it; names always sound warmer.","Reject it and log why: in a privacy-sensitive parent email 'your child' is clearer and safer than repeating the name.","Replace it with the student's full legal name and ID.","Ignore the suggestion without recording anything."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Rejecting against a privacy and audience constraint, and logging the reason, is the evidence trace.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_5_tone_ignores_audience","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_5_tone_bias_and_audience_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_tone_bias_and_audience","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_tone_bias_and_audience","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"For a parent audience, which replacement best fixes the pressuring word 'immediately'?","options":["right now","over the next two weeks","as soon as humanly possible","before it is too late"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A two-week window matches a partnership tone with parents; the others keep or increase the pressure.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_4_5_tone_ignores_audience","difficulty":"medium"}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_5_label_the_person","pattern":"labeling_instead_of_describing","description":"Using a character label ('lazy', 'careless') instead of describing the specific observed behavior","trigger":"draft assigns a trait to the person rather than naming what happened","feedback":"Replace labels with observed facts. 'Lazy habits' blames the child; 'could use more practice on fractions' describes the behavior.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_5_assumed_cause","pattern":"stating_unknown_cause_as_fact","description":"Asserting a cause you cannot verify ('obviously did not study') as if it were observed fact","trigger":"draft claims to know why something happened with no evidence","feedback":"State only what you can observe (the score, the date). You cannot know the cause, so do not assert it.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_4_5_tone_ignores_audience","pattern":"tone_mismatched_to_audience","description":"Keeping pressuring or urgent wording that fits a memo but alarms the actual audience (here, parents)","trigger":"word choice does not match who will read it","feedback":"Match tone to the audience. 'Fix this immediately' pressures parents; 'over the next two weeks' invites partnership.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"f1951c977c0f4edbc65877077a238781a7e077a2e3fcf54f0104bc97754bb264","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}